8 #edb/ref 17592186329854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329855 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186329857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1039 "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068697 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1045 "Determining Continuity from Graphs" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186069567 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068989 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "09c8fe50-cab2-5dfd-a90a-5053ffffc1bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069567 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069567 #edb/ref 1061 12020 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069567 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3561bec4-880a-5815-bc18-84c3d62a3191" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069567 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069568 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069568 #edb/ref 1061 12021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069568 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c9dde49-f9f9-5036-8e84-b7e1ced3796b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069568 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069569 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069569 #edb/ref 1061 12022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069569 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74cd2bd0-c1c2-5429-adda-d13aa18601d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069569 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069570 #edb/ref 1061 12023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069570 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64c70852-38a7-5f5e-ade8-36856f8eef67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069570 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069576 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069576 #edb/ref 1061 12404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069576 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c5269250-b2f9-525d-bfaa-9f0e6033687b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069576 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069958 #edb/ref 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is **continuous** over its entire domain if the graph of $y=f(x)$ can be drawn using a pencil and a piece of paper without taking the pencil off of the paper.\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function $y=x^2$ whose graph is shown below. \n\n![](images/67/67ffd0b78845693b522100f4f0ba460cae6fedd796964b24708e320eab04ce29.png)\n\nWe can draw as much of the function $y=x^2$ as we want without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, we say that $y=x^2$ is **continuous** at every point in its domain.\n\nLet's compare this to the function $y=g(x)$ below:\n\n![](images/a0/a07ccd15d14fa3eeb00a9c4ab07674c1c169e2a0d0a3e9fcb64798e9929e2ff9.png)\n\nNotice that this function has a jump in its value at $x=0.$\n\nIt is *impossible* to draw $y=g(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper to fill in the jump at $x=0.$ Therefore, we say that $y=g(x)$ has a **discontinuity** at $x=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069958 #edb/ref 1065 2583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069958 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1fb32bea-3d77-5134-936c-bfa9f0381fdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069958 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069961 #edb/ref 1064 "Now consider the function $y=h(x)$ below. This function has a discontinuity at $x=0,$ so it is not continuous over its entire domain.\n\n![](images/4e/4e41ca41e4befba33c62da68666a8c4a2e2c7a19c79331360ae4bf124edafd50.png)\n\nHowever, the function $h(x)$ may be continuous at other points, such as $x=1.$ \n\nTo determine whether $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1,$ we have to consider points in the **neighborhood** of $x=1.$ Roughly speaking, the *neighborhood of $x=1$* means the points on the curve that have an $x$-coordinate that's close to $1.$\n\nIndeed, in the *neighborhood* of $x=1,$ we can draw the function $h(x)$ without taking the pencil off the paper. So, $h(x)$ is indeed continuous at $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069961 #edb/ref 1065 2640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069961 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "54ca4bef-1b8e-5315-85d4-0667910b332c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069961 #edb/ref 1067 "Continuity at a Point" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238463 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238463 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238463 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e8bd6dd-1931-5df3-803b-dc0e5167142e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238721 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238721 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186238721 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4d8ed7c5-b2af-580b-8113-c798acb35065" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239321 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239321 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239321 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "92b58f29-e9d2-52ea-80a1-a85417e0693d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239932 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239932 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186239932 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d6b9cbc0-4187-50a5-81fd-c6539f6fe9ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240264 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240264 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240264 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f9aeed86-3876-53b5-b2e9-7b048f60598b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240445 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240445 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186238721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240445 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240445 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186239932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240445 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186240264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240445 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240445 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240445 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186240264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240445 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "42c90896-bfe8-504a-9692-0915b93a4eb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186240445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1069 "q-104051" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/65/65b9b32e8fac6a71141ce820855058367e9235129de7334db35a200905474cc0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc9393f2-809c-5769-b329-186102da51c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186240787 #edb/ref 1074 "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
